  "verbalization": "Trigohowilol G appears to exert its effects through a shared mechanism of action on T47D cells, which are targeted by the compound, and this target is ultimately linked to UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 2B4. The evidence suggests that Trigohowilol G may inhibit UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 2B4, as it is a common target of multiple compounds, including Terizidone and Crotaleschenine, which also interact with this enzyme. The final scores from the three paths indicate a consistent and plausible relationship, suggesting that Trigohowilol G may act as a competitive inhibitor of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 2B4, potentially modulating its activity in T47D cells. The presence of a well-documented mode of action—through the inhibition of a key enzyme in the drug metabolism pathway—supports the hypothesis that Trigohowilol G interacts with UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 2B4, contributing to its biological effects. The integration of multiple evidence paths reinforces the likelihood of a direct and mechanistically sound relationship between Trigohowilol G and UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 2B4.",
